{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass in the morning, etc.</b> Mahari wrote that dreams of the morning are more reliable; therefore ״וַתִּפָּעֶם רוּחוֹ״ — for he sensed that there was substance to them." "<b>And Pharaoh told them his dream, and there was none that interpreted them to Pharaoh.</b> ״חֲלֹמוֹ״ means a single dream, since it does not say ״חֲלֹמוֹתָיו״; ״וְאֵין פּוֹתֵר אוֹתָם״ has a plural sense, since it does not say ״אוֹתוֹ״. And it seems to me this is the explanation of Rashi, who said that they told him ״you will bury seven daughters,״ and it is difficult for me why Rashi did not also mention what is found (Genesis Rabbah 89:6) — that they told him ״you will conquer seven provinces, seven will rebel against you.״ Therefore it seems close in my eyes to explain that he wished to correct this shift in language: for Pharaoh told them חֲלֹמוֹ, his dream — because in his own mind it appeared that everything was one dream, and the matter of the dream's doubling was hidden from his eyes. Therefore it says ״וְאֵין פּוֹתֵר אוֹתָם לְפַרְעֹה״ — that they could not interpret them, that is, why the matter appeared to him twice and in different forms; this is the thing they could not interpret for him." "Another thing: the magicians gave him a reason for the doubling of the dream, because daughters have two purposes. One is that their purpose is for procreation and increase for their husbands. The second purpose is that they save their husbands from sin. For on this account a woman is likened to bread, as we explained above in parashat Vayeshev at the verse ״כִּי אִם הַלֶּחֶם אֲשֶׁר הוּא אוֹכֵל״ (Genesis 39:6), because there is a small organ in man that starves him; and specifically through relations of transgression, for the essence of relations for transgressors is such, and therefore his evil impulse starves him. But his wife satisfies him like bread that satisfies. And on account of this matter the magicians reasoned: surely these two dreams are one matter, and why did the dream come with such abundance of detail? Rather, surely he will beget seven daughters and give them in marriage to men and they will be fruitful and multiply, and this is the indication of the expression ״שֶׁבַע פָּרוֹת״ — on account of being fruitful and multiplying, for פָּרוֹת means being fruitful. And they will be to their husbands like bread made from the ears of grain, and this is the meaning of ״שֶׁבַע שִׁבֳּלִים.״ And according to this they interpreted well, but ״there was none that interpreted them״ — those two purposes — לְפַרְעֹה, for it was difficult for him why HaShem showed precisely such a thing to Pharaoh; surely many people beget daughters, so why did HaShem not show such to them? This is what it writes — that their voices did not enter his ears, meaning: why did HaShem make precisely such a thing heard in his ears and not in another's? According to Joseph's interpretation it fits well, for in the king's hand is the power to act and to repair the breach of the famine. This is what it writes: ״וְאֵין פּוֹתֵר אוֹתָם לְפַרְעֹה״ — that they could not interpret why HaShem showed these two purposes specifically to Pharaoh and did not show so to another, for in every man who gives his daughter in marriage these two purposes are found, and how does Pharaoh differ from another?" "But according to the midrash of the Ravrati, that they told him two interpretations — the daughters and the provinces — it seems fitting to explain that they understood that the ears of grain allude to daughters, along the lines of ״קִרְאֶן לוֹ וְיֹאכַל לָחֶם״ (Exodus 2:20), ״כִּי אִם הַלֶּחֶם אֲשֶׁר הוּא אוֹכֵל.״ And the cows allude to the conquest of provinces, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:17) ״בְּכוֹר שׁוֹרוֹ הָדָר לוֹ וְקַרְנֵי רְאֵם קַרְנָיו, בָּהֶם עַמִּים יְנַגַּח.״ For cows, when they are healthy and good, have the power of goring in their horns, and even though the bulls' strength is greater than the cows', nevertheless Scripture uses ״פָּרוֹת״ to teach a principle: for at the time of his success even the weak one, like a female, will say ״I am a mighty one״ and will prevail, and at the time of rebellion Pharaoh's strength will fail like a female, so that he will have no power against seven provinces that will rebel against him. And what is written ״וְאֵין פּוֹתֵר אוֹתָם״ — because Pharaoh thought it was one dream, therefore it says ״וַיְסַפֵּר לָהֶם חֲלֹמוֹ,״ and the magicians thought that they were two dreams, with an interpretation about the daughters — that he would bury them — and that seven provinces would rebel against him, and they did not want to tell Pharaoh this bad news. Therefore it says ״וְאֵין פּוֹתֵר אוֹתָם לְפַרְעֹה״ — specifically to Pharaoh himself they did not want to interpret, but in the presence of his other servants they did interpret it, and his servants too did not tell him anything from that interpretation. Therefore Pharaoh said to Joseph ״וְאֵין מַגִּיד לִי,״ and did not say ״וְאֵין פּוֹתֵר לִי״; rather, surely he saw his servants whispering and understood that they had interpreted for the bad, and therefore he complained about all the members of his household before Joseph, saying that of all of them there is none who tells him what the magicians interpreted in their presence." "Another thing: therefore he said ״וְאֵין מַגִּיד לִי,״ because Nebuchadnezzar did not forget his dream and said before his wise men that he had forgotten it, in order to test the truth — for whoever tells him the dream is surely a prophet and his interpretation is truth. But Pharaoh told his dream, and if so, by what would he know whether the interpretation was true? Therefore he would alter the telling of his dream in several details from what he had seen, and he reckoned that whoever would tell him the alterations — the spirit of HaShem spoke in him. And just as they told it before Joseph, so they told it also before the magicians, and no one told him the alterations, therefore Pharaoh did not accept their interpretation. But Joseph told him all the alterations and said to him: ״You did not see withered stalks but thin ones״ etc., as is found in the midrash (Tanchuma 3)."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:14.878272497Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}